{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TbpmgqjBSuU4fXCnmdZVGD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/*\n2\t* Author: Christian Huitema\n3\t* Copyright (c) 2022, Private Octopus, Inc.\n4\t* All rights reserved.\n5\t*\n6\t* Permission to use, copy, modify, and distribute this software for any\n7\t* purpose with or without fee is hereby granted, provided that the above\n8\t* copyright notice and this permission notice appear in all copies.\n9\t*\n10\t* THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS \"AS IS\" AND\n11\t* ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, THE IMPLIED\n12\t* WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE ARE\n13\t* DISCLAIMED. IN NO EVENT SHALL Private Octopus, Inc. BE LIABLE FOR ANY\n14\t* DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY, OR CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES\n15\t* (INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, PROCUREMENT OF SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR SERVICES;\n16\t* LOSS OF USE, DATA, OR PROFITS; OR BUSINESS INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER CAUSED AND\n17\t* ON ANY THEORY OF LIABILITY, WHETHER IN CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR TORT\n18\t* (INCLUDING NEGLIGENCE OR OTHERWISE) ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE OF THIS\n19\t* SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.\n20\t* \n21\t* The blocked port list used in this document is copied from the msquic\n22\t* implementation by Microsoft and from the list published by Cloudflare.\n23\t*/\n24\t\n25\t#include \"picoquic_internal.h\"\n26\t#include \"picoquic_unified_log.h\"\n27\t#include \"tls_api.h\"\n28\t#include <stdlib.h>\n29\t#include <string.h>\n30\t\n31\t/* Picoquic servers running over UDP can be both victims and enablers\n32\t * of reflection attacks.\n33\t *\n34\t * Servers may be targeted by DDOS attacks, which often use reflection\n35\t * through an unwitting UDP-based server to hide the actual source of\n36\t * the attack, and often to amplify the volume of the attack. The\n37\t * reflected attacks will appear as coming from the address and port\n38\t * of the server, such as NTP, DNS, other popular services, and also\n39\t * port number 0 in case of fragmented UDP datagrams. The code\n40\t * protect against those by just dropping packets from a list of\n41\t * such ports. This protection is described in this message to the\n42\t * HTTP WG list:\n43\t * https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/ietf-http-wg/2021JulSep/0053.html\n44\t * \n45\t * The blog entry https://blog.cloudflare.com/reflections-on-reflections/\n46\t * provides a list of UDP ports that Cloudflare saw used in\n47\t * reflection attacks:\n48\t * Count  Proto  Src port\n49\t *  3774   udp    123        NTP\n50\t *  1692   udp    1900       SSDP\n51\t *   438   udp    0          IP fragmentation\n52\t *   253   udp    53         DNS\n53\t *    42   udp    27015      SRCDS\n54\t *    20   udp    19         Chargen\n55\t *    19   udp    20800      Call Of Duty\n56\t *    16   udp    161        SNMP\n57\t *    12   udp    389        CLDAP\n58\t *    11   udp    111        Sunrpc\n59\t *    10   udp    137        Netbios\n60\t *     6   tcp    80         HTTP\n61\t *     5   udp    27005      SRCDS\n62\t *     2   udp    520        RIP\n63\t * \n64\t * Nick Banks at Microsoft pointed to the filtering list implemented\n65\t * in msquic:\n66\t * https://github.com/microsoft/msquic/blob/main/src/core/binding.c#L1399\n67\t * The list contains a different set than the one defined by cloudflare,\n68\t * with the inclusion of services like mDNS, NetBIOS, etc. \n69\t *       11211,  // memcache\n70\t *       5353,   // mDNS\n71\t *       1900,   // SSDP\n72\t *       500,    // IKE\n73\t *       389,    // CLDAP\n74\t *       161,    // SNMP\n75\t *       138,    // NETBIOS Datagram Service\n76\t *       137,    // NETBIOS Name Service\n77\t *       123,    // NTP\n78\t *       111,    // Portmap\n79\t *       53,     // DNS\n80\t *       19,     // Chargen\n81\t *       17,     // Quote of the Day\n82\t *       0,      // Unusable\n83\t * Services like mDNS or SSDp are typical local, and thus unlikely to be\n84\t * used for DDOS amplification. Attackers would have difficulties reaching\n85\t * these services from outside the local network. However, the attackers\n86\t * could forge the source address and cause the QUIC servers to bounce \n87\t * packets towards these services. This kind of \"request forgery attacks\"\n88\t * is discussed in section 21.5 of RFC 9000. Blocking the port numbers\n89\t * of servers targeted by such attacks provides a layer of protection.\n90\t * \n91\t * There are a couple of downsides to this protection:\n92\t * - Some of the ports listed here are part of the randomly assigned range,\n93\t *   and a unlucky client could end up using one of these ports.\n94\t * - Even if clients do not use a reserved port, NATs might. Not much recourse\n95\t *   there.\n96\t * - New vulnerable protocols are likely to be created in the future, which\n97\t *   means that the list will have to be updated.\n98\t * - If the server sits behind a firewall, the firewall might be a better\n99\t *   place for maintaining a list of blocked ports.\n100\t * \n101\t * The implementation provides teo mitigations against these downsides:\n102\t * \n103\t * - Servers can disable the protection if they don't want it.\n104\t * - Clients can test the port number assigned to their sockets and\n105\t *   pick a new one if they find a collision.\n106\t * \n107\t */\n108\t\n109\tconst uint16_t picoquic_blocked_port_list[] = {\n110\t        27015,  /* SRCDS */\n111\t        20800,  /* Call Of Duty */\n112\t        11211,  /* memcache */\n113\t        5353,   /* mDNS */\n114\t        1900,   /* SSDP */\n115\t        520,    /* RIP */\n116\t        500,    /* IKE */\n117\t        389,    /* CLDAP */\n118\t        161,    /* SNMP */\n119\t        138,    /* NETBIOS Datagram Service */\n120\t        137,    /* NETBIOS Name Service */\n121\t        123,    /* NTP */\n122\t        111,    /* Portmap -- used by SUN RPC */\n123\t        53,     /* DNS */\n124\t        19,     /* Chargen */\n125\t        17,     /* Quote of the Day */\n126\t        7,      /* Echo */\n127\t        0,      /* Unusable */\n128\t};\n129\t\n130\tconst size_t nb_picoquic_blocked_port_list = sizeof(picoquic_blocked_port_list) / sizeof(uint16_t);\n131\t\n132\tint picoquic_check_port_blocked(uint16_t port)\n133\t{\n134\t    int ret = 0;\n135\t\n136\t    for (size_t i = 0; i < nb_picoquic_blocked_port_list && port <= picoquic_blocked_port_list[i]; i++) {\n137\t        if (port == picoquic_blocked_port_list[i]){\n138\t            ret = 1;\n139\t            break;\n140\t        }\n141\t    }\n142\t\n143\t    return ret;\n144\t}\n145\t\n146\tint picoquic_check_addr_blocked(const struct sockaddr* addr_from)\n147\t{\n148\t    /* The sockaddr is always in network order. We must translate to\n149\t     * host order before performaing the check */\n150\t    uint16_t port = UINT16_MAX;\n151\t\n152\t    if (addr_from->sa_family == AF_INET) {\n153\t        port = ntohs(((struct sockaddr_in*)addr_from)->sin_port);\n154\t    }\n155\t    else if (addr_from->sa_family == AF_INET6) {\n156\t        /* configure an IPv6 sockaddr */\n157\t        port = ntohs(((struct sockaddr_in6*)addr_from)->sin6_port);\n158\t    }\n159\t    return picoquic_check_port_blocked(port);\n160\t}\n161\t\n162\tvoid picoquic_disable_port_blocking(picoquic_quic_t * quic, int is_port_blocking_disabled)\n163\t{\n164\t    PICOQUIC_THREAD_CHECK(quic);\n165\t    quic->is_port_blocking_disabled = is_port_blocking_disabled;\n166\t}\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2dc405c-b85c-47a3-92e4-53e1c53f419b","uuid":"7fdf14b1-b8a7-41e0-8bfd-9facf20995da","timestamp":"2026-05-07T08:11:22.166Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/picoquic/port_blocking.c","content":"/*\n* Author: Christian Huitema\n* Copyright (c) 2022, Private Octopus, Inc.\n* All rights reserved.\n*\n* Permission to use, copy, modify, and distribute this software for any\n* purpose with or without fee is hereby granted, provided that the above\n* copyright notice and this permission notice appear in all copies.\n*\n* THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS \"AS IS\" AND\n* ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, THE IMPLIED\n* WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE ARE\n* DISCLAIMED. IN NO EVENT SHALL Private Octopus, Inc. BE LIABLE FOR ANY\n* DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY, OR CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES\n* (INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, PROCUREMENT OF SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR SERVICES;\n* LOSS OF USE, DATA, OR PROFITS; OR BUSINESS INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER CAUSED AND\n* ON ANY THEORY OF LIABILITY, WHETHER IN CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR TORT\n* (INCLUDING NEGLIGENCE OR OTHERWISE) ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE OF THIS\n* SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.\n* \n* The blocked port list used in this document is copied from the msquic\n* implementation by Microsoft and from the list published by Cloudflare.\n*/\n\n#include \"picoquic_internal.h\"\n#include \"picoquic_unified_log.h\"\n#include \"tls_api.h\"\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n/* Picoquic servers running over UDP can be both victims and enablers\n * of reflection attacks.\n *\n * Servers may be targeted by DDOS attacks, which often use reflection\n * through an unwitting UDP-based server to hide the actual source of\n * the attack, and often to amplify the volume of the attack. The\n * reflected attacks will appear as coming from the address and port\n * of the server, such as NTP, DNS, other popular services, and also\n * port number 0 in case of fragmented UDP datagrams. The code\n * protect against those by just dropping packets from a list of\n * such ports. This protection is described in this message to the\n * HTTP WG list:\n * https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/ietf-http-wg/2021JulSep/0053.html\n * \n * The blog entry https://blog.cloudflare.com/reflections-on-reflections/\n * provides a list of UDP ports that Cloudflare saw used in\n * reflection attacks:\n * Count  Proto  Src port\n *  3774   udp    123        NTP\n *  1692   udp    1900       SSDP\n *   438   udp    0          IP fragmentation\n *   253   udp    53         DNS\n *    42   udp    27015      SRCDS\n *    20   udp    19         Chargen\n *    19   udp    20800      Call Of Duty\n *    16   udp    161        SNMP\n *    12   udp    389        CLDAP\n *    11   udp    111        Sunrpc\n *    10   udp    137        Netbios\n *     6   tcp    80         HTTP\n *     5   udp    27005      SRCDS\n *     2   udp    520        RIP\n * \n * Nick Banks at Microsoft pointed to the filtering list implemented\n * in msquic:\n * https://github.com/microsoft/msquic/blob/main/src/core/binding.c#L1399\n * The list contains a different set than the one defined by cloudflare,\n * with the inclusion of services like mDNS, NetBIOS, etc. \n *       11211,  // memcache\n *       5353,   // mDNS\n *       1900,   // SSDP\n *       500,    // IKE\n *       389,    // CLDAP\n *       161,    // SNMP\n *       138,    // NETBIOS Datagram Service\n *       137,    // NETBIOS Name Service\n *       123,    // NTP\n *       111,    // Portmap\n *       53,     // DNS\n *       19,     // Chargen\n *       17,     // Quote of the Day\n *       0,      // Unusable\n * Services like mDNS or SSDp are typical local, and thus unlikely to be\n * used for DDOS amplification. Attackers would have difficulties reaching\n * these services from outside the local network. However, the attackers\n * could forge the source address and cause the QUIC servers to bounce \n * packets towards these services. This kind of \"request forgery attacks\"\n * is discussed in section 21.5 of RFC 9000. Blocking the port numbers\n * of servers targeted by such attacks provides a layer of protection.\n * \n * There are a couple of downsides to this protection:\n * - Some of the ports listed here are part of the randomly assigned range,\n *   and a unlucky client could end up using one of these ports.\n * - Even if clients do not use a reserved port, NATs might. Not much recourse\n *   there.\n * - New vulnerable protocols are likely to be created in the future, which\n *   means that the list will have to be updated.\n * - If the server sits behind a firewall, the firewall might be a better\n *   place for maintaining a list of blocked ports.\n * \n * The implementation provides teo mitigations against these downsides:\n * \n * - Servers can disable the protection if they don't want it.\n * - Clients can test the port number assigned to their sockets and\n *   pick a new one if they find a collision.\n * \n */\n\nconst uint16_t picoquic_blocked_port_list[] = {\n        27015,  /* SRCDS */\n        20800,  /* Call Of Duty */\n        11211,  /* memcache */\n        5353,   /* mDNS */\n        1900,   /* SSDP */\n        520,    /* RIP */\n        500,    /* IKE */\n        389,    /* CLDAP */\n        161,    /* SNMP */\n        138,    /* NETBIOS Datagram Service */\n        137,    /* NETBIOS Name Service */\n        123,    /* NTP */\n        111,    /* Portmap -- used by SUN RPC */\n        53,     /* DNS */\n        19,     /* Chargen */\n        17,     /* Quote of the Day */\n        7,      /* Echo */\n        0,      /* Unusable */\n};\n\nconst size_t nb_picoquic_blocked_port_list = sizeof(picoquic_blocked_port_list) / sizeof(uint16_t);\n\nint picoquic_check_port_blocked(uint16_t port)\n{\n    int ret = 0;\n\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < nb_picoquic_blocked_port_list && port <= picoquic_blocked_port_list[i]; i++) {\n        if (port == picoquic_blocked_port_list[i]){\n            ret = 1;\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n\n    return ret;\n}\n\nint picoquic_check_addr_blocked(const struct sockaddr* addr_from)\n{\n    /* The sockaddr is always in network order. We must translate to\n     * host order before performaing the check */\n    uint16_t port = UINT16_MAX;\n\n    if (addr_from->sa_family == AF_INET) {\n        port = ntohs(((struct sockaddr_in*)addr_from)->sin_port);\n    }\n    else if (addr_from->sa_family == AF_INET6) {\n        /* configure an IPv6 sockaddr */\n        port = ntohs(((struct sockaddr_in6*)addr_from)->sin6_port);\n    }\n    return picoquic_check_port_blocked(port);\n}\n\nvoid picoquic_disable_port_blocking(picoquic_quic_t * quic, int is_port_blocking_disabled)\n{\n    PICOQUIC_THREAD_CHECK(quic);\n    quic->is_port_blocking_disabled = is_port_blocking_disabled;\n}\n","numLines":167,"startLine":1,"totalLines":167}}}
